1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,294
A charged comb bends a stream of water.

2
00:00:03,294 --> 00:00:04,982
Salt breaks into ions.

3
00:00:04,982 --> 00:00:07,115
Oil and water refuse to mix.

4
00:00:07,515 --> 00:00:10,033
Three puzzles, one hidden tug of war.

5
00:00:10,483 --> 00:00:12,642
Diya sees it at her kitchen tap.

6
00:00:12,642 --> 00:00:16,608
A rubbed plastic comb pulls the
thin stream of water towards it.

7
00:00:16,958 --> 00:00:21,951
Inside each water molecule, oxygen
pulls the shared electrons harder than

8
00:00:21,951 --> 00:00:22,851
hydrogen.

9
00:00:23,302 --> 00:00:27,479
By the end, you will know which atom
wins every tug of war, and by how

10
00:00:27,479 --> 00:00:28,379
much.

11
00:00:28,626 --> 00:00:30,828
So what is electronegativity?

12
00:00:31,178 --> 00:00:35,458
When two atoms bond, they share a
pair of electrons, and each atom pulls

13
00:00:35,458 --> 00:00:36,444
on that pair.

14
00:00:36,794 --> 00:00:41,559
Electronegativity is how strongly an
atom in a molecule pulls the shared

15
00:00:41,559 --> 00:00:43,391
electrons towards itself.

16
00:00:43,741 --> 00:00:46,460
We cannot measure it directly, like mass.

17
00:00:46,460 --> 00:00:48,399
We compare atoms on a scale.

18
00:00:48,999 --> 00:00:50,892
Look at hydrogen chloride.

19
00:00:50,892 --> 00:00:53,494
Hydrogen and chlorine share one pair.

20
00:00:53,894 --> 00:00:57,912
Chlorine pulls harder, so the
pair sits closer to chlorine.

21
00:00:57,912 --> 00:01:02,353
Chlorine gets a small negative
end, hydrogen a small positive end.

22
00:01:03,003 --> 00:01:05,526
Linus Pauling built the most used scale.

23
00:01:05,526 --> 00:01:09,891
He set fluorine, the strongest puller,
at four point zero, and compared

24
00:01:09,891 --> 00:01:11,592
every other atom with it.

25
00:01:12,242 --> 00:01:16,585
On our molecule bench, start with
period two, from lithium to fluorine.

26
00:01:16,935 --> 00:01:18,223
Lithium is big.

27
00:01:18,223 --> 00:01:23,287
Its outer electron sits far out,
and its pull is only one point zero.

28
00:01:23,687 --> 00:01:24,587
Move right.

29
00:01:24,558 --> 00:01:27,872
Each step adds a proton,
and the atom gets smaller.

30
00:01:28,272 --> 00:01:32,552
A smaller atom holds a shared
pair closer, so the pull grows.

31
00:01:32,552 --> 00:01:34,515
Beryllium, one point five.

32
00:01:34,515 --> 00:01:35,575
Boron, two.

33
00:01:35,575 --> 00:01:37,382
Carbon, two point five.

34
00:01:37,782 --> 00:01:39,072
Nitrogen, three.

35
00:01:39,072 --> 00:01:40,940
Oxygen, three point five.

36
00:01:40,940 --> 00:01:43,642
Fluorine, four, the strongest of all.

37
00:01:44,042 --> 00:01:45,813
Now down group seventeen.

38
00:01:45,813 --> 00:01:50,507
Fluorine four, chlorine three, bromine
two point eight, iodine two point

39
00:01:50,507 --> 00:01:51,407
five.

40
00:01:51,477 --> 00:01:53,206
Each step down adds a shell.

41
00:01:53,206 --> 00:01:56,218
The pair sits farther out,
so the pull gets weaker.

42
00:01:56,868 --> 00:02:01,973
So electronegativity increases across
a period, and decreases down a group,

43
00:02:01,973 --> 00:02:04,153
just like ionization enthalpy.

44
00:02:04,553 --> 00:02:05,745
Pause and think.

45
00:02:05,745 --> 00:02:10,386
Which bond has the bigger tug of war:
sodium with chlorine, or carbon with

46
00:02:10,386 --> 00:02:11,286
chlorine?

47
00:02:14,322 --> 00:02:15,840
Sodium with chlorine!

48
00:02:15,840 --> 00:02:17,748
The gap is two point one.

49
00:02:17,748 --> 00:02:20,804
Carbon with chlorine is
only zero point five.

50
00:02:21,454 --> 00:02:22,366
Now, why?

51
00:02:22,366 --> 00:02:23,852
One link at a time.

52
00:02:24,202 --> 00:02:25,102
Link one.

53
00:02:25,017 --> 00:02:29,766
Across a period, protons are added,
but the outer shell stays the same.

54
00:02:30,516 --> 00:02:31,430
Link two.

55
00:02:31,430 --> 00:02:33,091
So the atom shrinks.

56
00:02:33,091 --> 00:02:37,953
Lithium's radius is one hundred and
fifty two picometres, fluorine's only

57
00:02:37,953 --> 00:02:38,978
sixty four.

58
00:02:38,978 --> 00:02:42,947
A picometre is a millionth
of a millionth of a metre.

59
00:02:43,697 --> 00:02:44,597
Link three.

60
00:02:44,573 --> 00:02:49,418
So the shared pair sits closer to the
nucleus, and the attraction is stronger.

61
00:02:50,168 --> 00:02:51,068
Link four.

62
00:02:51,039 --> 00:02:53,933
Stronger attraction,
higher electronegativity.

63
00:02:53,933 --> 00:02:57,027
Down a group, every
link runs the other way.

64
00:02:57,777 --> 00:02:58,738
Last link.

65
00:02:58,738 --> 00:03:01,279
Strong pullers behave as non-metals.

66
00:03:01,279 --> 00:03:03,544
Weak pullers behave as metals.

67
00:03:04,294 --> 00:03:05,455
Remember this.

68
00:03:05,455 --> 00:03:10,471
Electronegativity is a tug of war for
shared electrons, and fluorine always

69
00:03:10,471 --> 00:03:11,371
wins.

70
00:03:11,779 --> 00:03:13,514
Now, one worked example.

71
00:03:13,814 --> 00:03:17,121
Carbon bonds with fluorine,
oxygen and chlorine.

72
00:03:17,121 --> 00:03:20,219
Which bond shares its
electrons most unevenly?

73
00:03:20,719 --> 00:03:24,248
Carbon is two point five,
fluorine four point zero.

74
00:03:24,248 --> 00:03:26,208
The gap is one point five.

75
00:03:26,958 --> 00:03:29,049
Oxygen is three point five.

76
00:03:29,049 --> 00:03:30,799
Gap, one point zero.

77
00:03:31,549 --> 00:03:33,708
Chlorine is three point zero.

78
00:03:33,708 --> 00:03:35,814
Gap, only zero point five.

79
00:03:36,564 --> 00:03:38,877
Bigger gap, more uneven sharing.

80
00:03:38,877 --> 00:03:43,771
So carbon fluorine comes first, then
carbon oxygen, then carbon chlorine.

81
00:03:43,771 --> 00:03:46,619
Carbon always gets the small positive end.

82
00:03:47,269 --> 00:03:49,773
Period three follows the same rule.

83
00:03:49,773 --> 00:03:51,671
Sodium, zero point nine.

84
00:03:51,671 --> 00:03:53,623
Magnesium, one point two.

85
00:03:53,623 --> 00:03:55,628
Aluminium, one point five.

86
00:03:55,628 --> 00:03:57,580
Silicon, one point eight.

87
00:03:57,980 --> 00:03:59,846
Phosphorus, two point one.

88
00:03:59,846 --> 00:04:01,614
Sulphur, two point five.

89
00:04:01,614 --> 00:04:02,868
Chlorine, three.

90
00:04:03,268 --> 00:04:05,352
Low values belong to metals.

91
00:04:05,352 --> 00:04:07,650
High values belong to non-metals.

92
00:04:08,050 --> 00:04:09,176
One more fact.

93
00:04:09,176 --> 00:04:13,641
An atom's value shifts a little with
its bonding partner, so tables give

94
00:04:13,641 --> 00:04:14,757
typical values.

95
00:04:15,407 --> 00:04:16,873
Now, the exam trap.

96
00:04:17,223 --> 00:04:22,459
Chlorine releases more energy than fluorine
when a lone atom gains an electron.

97
00:04:22,459 --> 00:04:25,844
So students call chlorine
the most electronegative.

98
00:04:26,244 --> 00:04:27,144
Wrong.

99
00:04:26,860 --> 00:04:30,183
That is electron gain
enthalpy, for a free atom.

100
00:04:30,183 --> 00:04:34,798
Electronegativity is the pull inside
a bond, and fluorine is highest, at

101
00:04:34,798 --> 00:04:35,698
four.

102
00:04:36,014 --> 00:04:37,554
Where is this asked?

103
00:04:37,554 --> 00:04:39,311
Both JEE Main and NEET.

104
00:04:39,311 --> 00:04:44,013
Ordering across a period, and statement
questions with oxidation states.

105
00:04:44,613 --> 00:04:45,831
Exam level now.

106
00:04:45,831 --> 00:04:50,900
On the Pauling scale, which pair has the
largest electronegativity difference?

107
00:04:50,900 --> 00:04:52,170
Pause and solve.

108
00:04:57,570 --> 00:04:58,924
The answer is C.

109
00:04:58,924 --> 00:05:00,581
Magnesium and chlorine.

110
00:05:01,081 --> 00:05:04,126
All four contain chlorine,
at three point zero.

111
00:05:04,126 --> 00:05:06,931
So the weakest puller
gives the biggest gap.

112
00:05:07,431 --> 00:05:10,542
Magnesium sits furthest
left, at one point two.

113
00:05:10,542 --> 00:05:12,459
Its gap is one point eight.

114
00:05:12,959 --> 00:05:17,343
Silicon gives one point two,
carbon and sulphur zero point five.

115
00:05:17,343 --> 00:05:20,978
B traps students who forget
magnesium is further left.

116
00:05:21,578 --> 00:05:23,039
Here is your recap.

117
00:05:23,039 --> 00:05:24,540
Screenshot this card.

118
00:05:27,740 --> 00:05:30,577
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

119
00:05:30,977 --> 00:05:31,877
One.

120
00:05:31,502 --> 00:05:34,770
Why is fluorine more
electronegative than lithium?

121
00:05:35,570 --> 00:05:36,470
Two.

122
00:05:36,122 --> 00:05:39,938
Why does electronegativity
fall from fluorine to iodine?

123
00:05:40,738 --> 00:05:41,638
Three.

124
00:05:41,384 --> 00:05:45,017
Chlorine has the most negative
electron gain enthalpy.

125
00:05:45,017 --> 00:05:47,941
Why is fluorine still
more electronegative?

126
00:05:48,841 --> 00:05:53,684
Next time: how can oxygen be plus
two in one compound and minus two in

127
00:05:53,684 --> 00:05:54,584
another?

128
00:05:54,440 --> 00:05:57,335
Find out in valency and oxidation states.
